A core dimension of intelligence is learning how to optimize learning and thinking under constraints of architecture, compute, and data resources. There are numerous challenges to solve in the pursuit of such “bounded optimality.” One question and opportunity is: “What should be remembered and recalled?” We’ve just published our paper on one piece of the memory challenge—on the effective compression of test-time reflection to reduce the size of context while keeping an eye on the coherence of the string of contextual memory. Read more here about our Memento project. Enjoyed the collaboration!
@MSFTResearch @vkontonis @DimitrisPapail
Quoting @DimitrisPapail

clean_summary:
LLMs generate a lot of tokens while thinking but struggle to manage the growing context on their own. Memento teaches models to chunk their reasoning into blocks and compress each into a dense summary called a memento. After writing a memento, the original block is deleted from memory, cutting peak memory usage by 2 to 3 times and nearly doubling serving throughput within a single generation call.

Training employs a three-stage curriculum, requiring only about 30,000 examples for models to learn this skill. The accuracy drop is minimal and mainly arises from training on another model's traces rather than from compression itself. Reinforcement learning and scaling to larger models help close the remaining gap. Deleted blocks do not completely vanish, as their information leaks forward through the KV cache representations; removing this hidden channel by restarting the KV cache results in a 15-point accuracy drop, highlighting its significance. The next step involves agents, where selectively remembering and forgetting across many turns presents the real bottleneck.

@DimitrisPapail: "tldr"

Another impressive paper from Microsoft. It teaches reasoning models to compress their own chain-of-thought mid-generation. The most interesting finding isn't just the 2-3x memory savings or the doubled throughput; it's that when the model erases a reasoning block after summarizing it, the deleted information continues to leak forward through the KV cache representations. This forms an implicit second channel that accounts for 15 percentage points of accuracy.

The model is, in a meaningful sense, remembering things it can no longer see. If context management turns out to be a teachable skill, and 30,000 training examples seem to be sufficient, then the bottleneck for long-horizon agents may be less about architecture and more about the right training data, which presents a very different kind of problem than most people are currently addressing.

@DimitrisPapail: "..."

Very excited that Microsoft is using our dataset OpenThoughts and summarizes it in a super clever way to make reasoning much more efficient. Most people do not understand how verbose reasoning models can get; they often produce 30,000 tokens to answer one math question, which is one-third of a Harry Potter novel. In earlier research, we tried summarizing the reasoning traces and fine-tuning on that, but it killed reasoning performance.

Microsoft implemented many clever tricks to break the reasoning traces into pieces using dynamic programming and summarized them separately into self-contained nuggets they called mementos. They released these compactified reasoning traces in a new dataset called OpenMementos, which is six times more compact on average. Very cool work on efficient reasoning.

@erichorvitz: "A core dimension of intelligence is learning how to optimize learning and thinking under constraints of architecture, compute, and data resources. There are numerous challenges to solve in the pursuit of such 'bounded optimality.' One question and opportunity is: 'What should be remembered and recalled?' We’ve just published our paper on one piece of the memory challenge—on the effective compression of test-time reflection to reduce the size of context while keeping an eye on the coherence of the string of contextual memory. Read more here about our Memento project. Enjoyed the collaboration!"

Found something in my daily use of Claude Code that validates our Memento results: Claude Code flushes the KV cache after some idle period, and when I come back past that, the model is noticeably harder to work with.

The conjecture is that post-flush, the model is no longer continuing its trajectory. It's shoved into a weird out-of-distribution regime where it has to simulate what has happened from the tokens and resume from a reconstruction, which is much harder than just continuing. We measured this effect in our paper, showing that KV states (soft embeddings) carry information that text tokens don't, even when attention is masked. The bottom line is that if you flush your cache, you lose a lot of accuracy.

